The Problem: The Copy-Paste Disaster and The Broken Sequence

Imagine your company extracts data from different files to generate daily reports.
You are tasked with writing a CSV reader and a PDF reader.

The Junior Developer writes two separate classes: CsvMiner and PdfMiner.
Both classes have to:

    Open a connection to the server.
    Read the file.
    Parse the specific data (This is the only part that is actually different!).
    Analyze the data.
    Save the report to the database.
    Close the connection.

The Architectural Danger:

    Code Duplication: Steps 1, 4, 5, and 6 are copy-pasted into both classes. If the database URL changes, you have to
    fix it in 50 different files.
    The Broken Sequence: A junior developer might write a new XmlMiner tomorrow and accidentally forget to call Step 6
    (Close the connection). The server runs out of memory and crashes.

The Senior Solution: The Template Method (The Hollywood Principle)
The Template Method Pattern introduces the "Hollywood Principle": Don't call us, we'll call you.
Instead of letting the child classes decide what happens, we create a powerful Parent class. The Parent class locks in
the exact step-by-step sequence of the algorithm (The Template). The child classes are only allowed to fill in
the blanks.